Z||000003||Archie||10-07-2000||12:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>How about these?  On ebay right now for $499, ends tomorrow.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Read the EBay ad carefully. That is not one of my exhaust systems. It is not similar to one of my exhaust systems. It was built by someone assembling one of my kits. <P>It may fit just fine... it may work just fine, but I don't know.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||10-07-2000||01:04 AM|||| Hey Archie...thanks for your input!<BR>What I found out -  a phone call to you might have solved...<P>1. SANDERSON HEADERS - PART #CC90 $219.00<P>2. RAM HORNS - Yes those off early 60's cars and chev trucks. If you are lucky you can find them at a bone yard for $35/pc up to $125/pc. Depending where you go. I think Archie uses these for a reason. I got a set today for $75 and i can see that they would work perfect.<P>As for the exhaust above. It's very nice. Ready to go. One of the pipes seems to be running over the tranny which I don't particularly like.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Zorba||10-07-2000||01:16 AM|||| Here is a picture describing the RAM HORNS A a little better. I got the second one with the larger measurement.<P> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/manifold.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Archie||10-07-2000||08:19 AM||||Yep, I use the Manifolds for s reason. The reason I've used the "Ram Horn" manifolds for so many years is that they disapate heat at a much slower rate compared to headers. Thus keeping the engine compartment cooler. If you are using a carburator with headers on a hot day, I've seen  times where shutting the car off could set you up for a little vapor lock or even fuel perculation. The Ram Horn manifolds also are a lot quieter than the CC-90 headers. This difference in noise level could be the difference in wether your neighbors burn your house down or not.<P>The CC-90 headers fit pretty good and work fine. I've used them like 10 times already this year but mostly on fuel injected cars where vapor lock is unlikely to occur.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000007||Philphine||10-07-2000||09:28 AM||||do you giveup much performance wise compared to headers? is it worth it to extrude hone them?||||reg||1
Z||000008||V8Fiero||10-07-2000||01:29 PM||||I was lucky enough to get a set of ram horns from a buddy of mine for $50. One of them was angle about 45* to the right. That came in handy when trying to clear my automatic transmission with the exhaust.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierov8||10-08-2000||12:40 AM||||Archie,<P>you probably know the answer to this one:  are the ram horn manifolds and the Sanderson CC90's dimensionally the same?  Can you use one in place of the other?  <P>Do you use a single exhaust setup or different ones based on if you are installing the rams horns or the CC90's?<P>thx!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||Archie||10-08-2000||07:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierov8:<BR><B>Archie, you probably know the answer to this one:  are the ram horn manifolds and the Sanderson CC90's dimensionally the same?  Can you use one in place of the other?  <P>Do you use a single exhaust setup or different ones based on if you are installing the rams horns or the CC90's?<BR>thx!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have not actually measured both parts to campare them but, they are REAL CLOSE to being the same. You can use my regular dual exhaust set-up but you need to modify the upper exh. pipe to fit to the header collecter.<P>To answer another question.....<P>I think the HP difference between the headers and the manifolds would be 20 to 25 HP. & yes Honing would help the flow somewhat on the manifolds.<P>Archie ||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||10-08-2000||11:03 AM||||Good info on the headers here.  <P>Ya know, that one exhaust on Ebay, it makes me think that if you put a turbo in where the two banks join....hmmmmmmmmmm.....||||reg||1
